<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black Sea</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Black Sea is an inland sea between southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, connected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a> and Dardanelles. It was a vital trade and strategic zone for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> long controlled its shores. Russian expansion to the Black Sea coast in the eighteenth century was a major gain for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-sea.jpg" alt="Black Sea" width="320" height="287" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The SeaWiFS Project · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Danube_Spills_into_the_Black_Sea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
